@@s001 Reducing to a flow: recipe and gadget
@@s002 dato:other nodes,ora:v split,no:passage the gadget prevents
@@s003 I want the node <b>v</b> to be used by <b>at most one path</b>. The flow puts capacities on the <b>arcs</b>, not on the nodes.
@@s004 I split v into two nodes: <b>v_in</b> receives the incoming arcs, <b>v_out</b> emits the outgoing ones, and the arc <b>v_in → v_out</b> has the capacity of the node: 1.
@@s005 Now all the flow that passes through v must cross the arc of capacity 1: it cannot exceed 1. The constraint on the node has become a constraint on the arc, and the max flow knows how to solve it.
@@s006 Reducing to a flow: recipe and gadget
@@s007 The flow puts capacities on the arcs, but the constraints usually concern the nodes: a node used at most once, or a vertex to remove. The split gadget turns a node constraint into an arc constraint.
@@s008 Splitting the node
@@s009 Replace the node <code>v</code> with two nodes <code>v_in</code> and <code>v_out</code>, joined by <code>v_in → v_out</code> with capacity <code>c(v)</code>. The arcs that entered <code>v</code> now enter <code>v_in</code>; those that left it leave from <code>v_out</code>.
@@s010 Every flow that passes through <code>v</code> must cross that arc: so it cannot exceed <code>c(v)</code>. The cost doubles the nodes and adds <code>n</code> arcs: same order of magnitude.
@@s011 The node v becomes two nodes with an arc of capacity 1
@@s012 Where it is used
@@s013 Paths <b>node-disjoint</b> (each node used by only one path), <b>minimum number of nodes</b> to remove to separate s from t (node cut), and "special" nodes that cannot be removed (infinite capacity).
@@s014 The recipe: reduce to a flow
@@s015 For each problem you want to solve with the flow, answer five questions in order. It is the same recipe for matching, pairs and disjoint paths:
@@s016 <b>The nodes</b>: which and how many, as a function of the parameters of the problem. Usually: <code>s</code>, <code>t</code>, one node for each object of type A, one for each object of type B.
@@s017 <b>The arcs</b>: from where to where, oriented, and how many in total.
@@s018 <b>The capacities</b>: each with its meaning. Capacity 1 means "at most once". Capacity <code>∞</code> means "no limit, but the arc cannot be cut".
@@s019 <b>The meaning of the flow</b>: what a maximum flow means in the original problem, and what it is worth. It must be proved in both directions: from a solution a flow arises, and from an integer flow a solution arises.
@@s020 <b>The time</b>: which flow algorithm and with which parameters. Ford-Fulkerson costs O(m·C), where <code>m</code> are the arcs and <code>C</code> the maximum value of the flow.
@@s021 Order of work: first draw the example graph, precisely on nodes, arcs and how many they are. Then write the general rule. Whoever skips the drawing almost always gets a capacity wrong.
@@s022 At the exam
@@s023 At the exam
@@s024 Constraint "a node at most once" → split of the node with capacity 1. Phrase: <i>"Split each node v into v_in and v_out joined by an arc of capacity 1; arcs into v enter v_in, arcs out of v leave v_out."</i>
